{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789906717,"nonce":"yyHgLk6raT3/ukbIOm+kJUSJQeg=","title":"The collector keeps a dead list alive from a stale stack word on float","message":"Speed-center float.py, tiered with LLVM, NUC x86_64. Every collection\nafter the first marks about 6.2 MB with the outlined-resume-point build\nand about 2.1 MB with the build before it (185882e), the extra being\none complete points list of a finished benchmark(n) call: its header,\nits 1 MB element buffer and up to 100k Point objects. Marking it costs\n3 to 4 ms per collection against 0.5 ms, one collection every four\niterations, so a 3.1 ms iteration reads as 3.8 ms in the mean while the\nmedian and the minimum are unchanged. Minor faults rise from 23k to 51k\nover 300 iterations as the dead objects' pages are re-touched.\n\nWhat holds it: a word of the machine stack, in the innermost few\nhundred bytes above the collector's spill area at collection time, an\ninterior pointer (header+8, the length field's address, which LICM\nhoists) to the previous call's list. ZYNTAX_TRACE_GC=2 attributes it:\nthe stack window reaches 6.6 MB, every register-file root under 1 MB.\nThe tier-1 HIR of benchmark is identical in both builds, so the LLVM\nframe is the same; what differs is the frame that ran the first call\n(the outlined region against the baseline resume point) and the Rust\nframes below the JIT code, which differ between binaries. Shifting the\nstack by up to 250 bytes of environment does not change it, so it is\nnot alignment luck; it is which dead slot a live frame leaves unwritten.\nNeither build releases points: it escapes into maximize, which returns\none of its elements, so both leave it to the collector; the difference\nis only whether a stale word names it.\n\nTried: zeroing 16 KB of stack below the interpreter's frame after the\ntransferred frame returns (the region's frame lies there) changed\nnothing, so the word is above that. Keeping collect_from out of\ncollect's frame (inline(never)) changed nothing either, kept anyway.\n\nOnly float shows it: meteor's collections mark the same in both builds,\nthe other kernels never collect at 30 iterations.\n\nWays out: a per-call release of points, which needs the callee's\nparameter to be known borrowed even though an element is returned\n(drop_insert's derived-values rule treats the loaded element as another\nname for the list); or scanning JIT frames precisely (stack maps) instead\nof conservatively.","files":null}]}